# .env -- where this repository's upstream sources are cloned on this machine.
#
# Copy to `.env` and edit. **`.env.template` would be gitignored** - the
# `.gitignore` rule is `.env.*` with `!.env.example` carved out - so this file
# is the one that travels.
#
# Nothing in here is a secret, and that is what separates it from the `.env`
# this tool writes into a CUSTOMER repository. That one holds design-time
# database credentials and is described in
# `internal/scaffold/templates/.env.example`. The two are unrelated: this file
# is for somebody editing asgard-fde-cli.
#
# **The URL is the source of truth; where you clone it is not.** That is why
# these are variables rather than paths written into the scripts - the paths
# were, and they were true on one machine and wrong on every other, which is
# how `go run ./hack tables` went eight upstream commits without being run.
#
#   go run ./hack sources    what each of these resolves to, and how far behind it is
#
# Nothing reads these to clone or pull. `git -C <path> pull` is the reader's
# act: a script that fetched would turn "read at this commit" into "read at
# whatever was there when the script ran", which is the one thing the
# provenance rule exists to prevent.

# The CRDs - the platform contract. `go run ./hack tables` holds internal/gate's
# pinned enum and constraint tables against these.
#   https://github.com/asgard-ai-platform/asgard-kube
ASGARD_KUBE=~/projects/asgard/asgard-kube

# The product documentation, which is what the wiki is written from.
#   https://github.com/asgard-ai-platform/asgard-docs
ASGARD_DOCS=~/projects/asgard-docs

# The processor definitions the CRDs are generated from. internal/gate's
# processor contract is a pinned copy of that repository's ProcessorDefinitions.
#   https://github.com/asgard-ai-platform/asgard-core   (private)
ASGARD_CORE=~/projects/asgard/asgard-core
